---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LlnaRule from '../../components/LlnaRule.astro';
import LlnaRun from '../../components/LlnaRun.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
const paper = (await getCollection('publications')).find((p) => p.id === 'life-on-graphs')!.data;
const doi = `https://doi.org/${paper.doi}`;
// One tab per theme; the hash picks the tab, so a theme can be linked.
const tabs = [
  { id: 'rules', name: 'Rules' },
  { id: 'firing-squad', name: 'Firing squad' },
  { id: 'majority', name: 'Majority' },
  { id: 'defects', name: 'Defects' },
];
---
<Base title="Life on graphs" description="Conway's Game of Life on networks, from my Physica D paper: the interval rule diagram and the mean-field curve, a firing squad and a majority vote solved bottom-up, and defects spreading on lattices, small worlds and random networks." wide>
  <div class="measure">
    <h1>Life on graphs</h1>
    <p>
      Conway's Game of Life, taken off its grid and onto networks, where a cell's neighbours are whoever it
      happens to be wired to. This is the demo of <a href={doi}>{paper.title}</a>, with Lucas Caldeira de
      Oliveira, Odemir Bruno and Jan Baetens (<i>{paper.venue}</i>, {paper.year}). The first theme defines
      the rules and the curve that predicts them; the next two use it to solve two classic problems of
      collective computation; the last follows a single flipped node.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabbed">
    <nav class="tabs" aria-label="Themes">
      <ul role="tablist" aria-orientation="vertical">
        {tabs.map((t, i) => <li><a role="tab" id={`tab-${t.id}`} href={`#${t.id}`} aria-controls={t.id} aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} tabindex={i === 0 ? 0 : -1}>{t.name}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="panels">

      <section role="tabpanel" id="rules" aria-labelledby="tab-rules" tabindex="0">
        <div class="measure">
          <h2>Life-like rules on networks</h2>
          <p>
            Conway's Game of Life counts a cell's live neighbours and answers differently depending on the
            cell's own state: a dead cell with exactly three live neighbours is
            born, a live cell with two or three survives, everything else dies or stays dead. Rules of that
            shape are called <em>outer-totalistic</em>: what happens depends on your own state and on the
            total of your neighbours, not on which ones. On a grid every cell has eight neighbours, so
            "exactly three" is a clear instruction. On a network, where one node has two neighbours and the
            next has twenty, it is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            The fix, worked out in <a href={doi}>the paper</a> (code on <a href="https://github.com/mrollier/essential-metrics-life-on-graphs">GitHub</a>),
            is to
            count the <em>share</em> rather than the number: ρ, the density of live nodes among a node's
            neighbours, a number between 0 and 1. Cut the unit interval into <em>r</em> equal pieces and let
            the rule say, for each piece, whether a dead node is born there and whether a live node survives
            there. A rule is then a pair of sets of intervals, born <em>B</em> and survive <em>S</em>:
          </p>
          <p class="formula">
            ϕ(s, ρ) = 1 if s = 0 and ρ ∈ ⋃B; 1 if s = 1 and ρ ∈ ⋃S; 0 otherwise.
          </p>
          <p>
            With r = 9 on a grid of eight neighbours the nine intervals match the nine possible counts 0 to 8
            exactly, and Life is back: B = {'{'}R<sub>3</sub>{'}'}, S = {'{'}R<sub>2</sub>, R<sub>3</sub>{'}'}. We call the whole family <em>Life-like network automata</em>. There are 2<sup>2r</sup> rules for each resolution r, so 262,144 at r = 9.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two details make this the right definition and not merely a convenient one. First, r must be odd,
            so that one interval sits in the middle, symmetric around ρ = ½. Second, the intervals are
            half-open in a mirrored way: below the middle each one includes its left end,
            [<i>i</i>/r, (<i>i</i>+1)/r[; above the middle each includes its right end,
            ]<i>i</i>/r, (<i>i</i>+1)/r]; and the middle one is closed at both ends. The dots on the axis of the
            diagram mark which side each boundary belongs to. This is what makes swapping every on and off a
            symmetry of the family, as it is for cellular automata: a dead node seeing density ρ becomes a live
            node seeing 1 − ρ, and that must land in the mirror-image interval, boundaries included. On a
            network with small degrees a density lands exactly on a boundary all the time, so without the
            mirroring the symmetry would break.
          </p>
          <p>
            A rule is written ϕ<sup>r</sup><sub>β,σ</sub>, with β the born set read as a binary number, interval
            <i>i</i> worth 2<sup><i>i</i></sup>, and σ the same for the survive set. Life is ϕ<sup>9</sup><sub>8,12</sub>: born 2<sup>3</sup> = 8,
            survive 2<sup>2</sup> + 2<sup>3</sup> = 12. Because of the symmetry every rule has an <em>equivalent</em> one that does
            the same thing with the colours swapped, and some rules are their own equivalent; the readout
            under the diagram says which. Click any cell of the diagram to change the rule, or type β and σ.
          </p>
          <p>
            Under the diagram is the <em>mean-field curve</em> for a node of degree k: if a share ρ of all
            nodes is on and there is no further structure, what share do we expect after one tick? Sum over the
            possible counts q of live neighbours, each weighted by the binomial share of neighbourhoods that
            produce it, and the answer follows from the diagram alone. The dashed lines through the diagram show
            which densities q/k a node of degree k can see, and the circles how common each is. The curve's
            value at ρ = ½, marked in blue, is the <em>Hamming weight</em>, the share of all inputs that yield a
            live node. Where the curve crosses the diagonal the density would stay put; where it crosses the
            anti-diagonal it would flip to its complement. That is all we need to go hunting for a rule.
          </p>
        </div>
        <LlnaRule />
        <div class="measure">
          <p>
            Things to try. Slide the degree from 4 to 12 with HighLife selected and watch the mean-field curve
            move while the diagram stays put: which intervals a node can reach depends on its degree, and so
            does its behaviour, which is the whole point of doing this on networks. Pick the firing squad rule
            and see a curve that crosses the anti-diagonal at ½ and nowhere else. Set r to 3 and the family
            shrinks to a handful of threshold rules, one of them rule 150, the parity rule that counts a node's own state too.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="firing-squad" aria-labelledby="tab-firing-squad" tabindex="0" hidden>
        <div class="measure">
          <h2>The firing squad</h2>
          <p>
            The <em>firing squad synchronisation problem</em>: from a random start, get every node to switch
            on and off in step, all on, all off, all on, for ever. Each node sees only its neighbours; nobody
            sees the whole. It is a classic for cellular automata, where a line of cells needs at least five
            states per cell to be solved exactly. With two states and an arbitrary network the honest question
            is how often a rule manages it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The usual route is a genetic algorithm: breed rules, keep the winners, and accept a solution you
            cannot explain. The paper goes bottom-up instead and asks what the mean-field curve of a good
            solver must look like. Two conditions. The rule must be its own equivalent, since the goal looks
            the same with the colours swapped. And the curve must lie above the anti-diagonal 1 − ρ to the
            left of ½ and below it to the right, so that ½ is an unstable balance: every tick then pushes the
            density further from ½ while flipping it. At degree 8 and r = 9 that leaves 27 candidates out of
            262,144, and running those settles on one, ϕ<sup>9</sup><sub>23,47</sub>: born in R<sub>0</sub>, R<sub>1</sub>, R<sub>2</sub> and R<sub>4</sub>,
            survive in R<sub>0</sub> to R<sub>3</sub> and R<sub>5</sub>. Its steep curve sends the density to 0 or 1 within a
            few ticks, and its high sensitivity to single flips keeps it from freezing in a patchy state.
          </p>
          <p>
            On the paper's networks, 900 nodes with 3,600 links, rewired from a lattice of eight neighbours,
            it synchronises 84% of random starts, and up to 93% when the rewiring probability is near 0.32.
            Two things surprised us. Mean degree 7 fails far more often than 8 or 9. And a fully rewired
            network does worse than a small world: some shortcuts help, all shortcuts do not. The figure below
            reproduces the setting at any size up to the paper's: pick a topology, a start, a rule, and press
            play. The trace shows the share of nodes that are on; a solved run ends in a square wave between 0
            and 1, and the run pauses itself once the verdict is in. The trials button repeats the experiment on
            twenty fresh networks and starts with the same settings.
          </p>
        </div>
        <LlnaRun mode="fssp" />
        <div class="measure">
          <p>
            Things to try. Set the rewiring to 0 for the bare lattice: the squad mostly fails, stuck with
            islands that flash out of step. Slide it up and watch the success rate climb, then fall again past
            0.8. Switch to four neighbours, or to the random and scale-free families, where the degree varies
            far more. Move the start share towards 0 or 1 and the job gets easier, as it should. Then change
            the rule with a single click on the diagram and run the trials again: the criteria are strict, and
            most neighbouring rules do nothing of the kind.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="majority" aria-labelledby="tab-majority" tabindex="0" hidden>
        <div class="measure">
          <h2>The majority</h2>
          <p>
            The <em>majority problem</em>, also called density classification: from a random start, decide
            whether more nodes were on or off, and have every node settle on the answer. Again nobody sees the
            whole. And again the mean-field curve says what a solver must look like: instead of flipping the
            density each tick we want to freeze it, so 0 and 1 must be stable and ½ unstable, which is the
            firing squad's curve turned upside down. Complementing both sets does exactly that, (B, S) →
            (B<sup>c</sup>, S<sup>c</sup>), and turns ϕ<sup>9</sup><sub>23,47</sub> into ϕ<sup>9</sup><sub>488,464</sub>: a node
            is born when its neighbourhood is mostly on and survives unless it is mostly off.
          </p>
          <p>
            The paper only remarks on this, with a warning: the rules it produces often get the answer wrong.
            The figure shows why. The race to a homogeneous state is quick but local, so a network where 45% of
            the nodes are on can freeze on the wrong answer in one region and export it. What the rule does
            reliably is steer the network to <em>some</em> homogeneous state, and which one it picks turns out
            to say something about the network itself, which is where my more recent work takes it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <LlnaRun mode="majority" preset="majority" rho={0.4} />
        <div class="measure">
          <p>
            Things to try. Start at 40% on and at 60% on, and check the verdict. Then move the start share
            towards 50% and run twenty trials at each step: the success rate drops to a coin toss well before
            the majority is thin. Compare the lattice with the random network, and the rule with the plain
            majority vote you can build by hand, born and survive both filled from ½ upwards.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="defects" aria-labelledby="tab-defects" tabindex="0" hidden>
        <div class="measure">
          <h2>Defects</h2>
          <p>
            The other half of the paper's toolkit is sensitivity: what a single flipped node does. Run the same
            automaton twice from starts that differ in one node and count, at every tick, the nodes where the
            two runs disagree. That share is the <em>defect density</em> δ, drawn in blue under the network;
            the origin wears a thick ring and every node that currently differs a thin one. A rule that damps
            perturbations lets the rings die out; a chaotic one spreads them over the whole network and keeps
            them there. On the rule diagram that is the difference between smooth born and survive sets and
            jagged ones, and the paper turns it into a number, the Boolean sensitivity, that predicts δ well.
          </p>
          <p>
            The example here is the Morley rule, ϕ<sup>9</sup><sub>328,52</sub>, which the paper uses because its
            behaviour depends so visibly on the setting: on the lattice it converges gradually, on a small world
            suddenly, and on a random network sometimes not at all, and both the start share and the rewiring
            show sharp transitions in between. A single rule, and its phenotype is a property of the network
            as much as of the rule.
          </p>
        </div>
        <LlnaRun mode="defect" preset="morley" />
        <div class="measure">
          <p>
            Things to try. Morley on the bare lattice at 50% on, then at 25% and 75%. Add rewiring and look for
            the point where the defect stops healing. Then switch to the firing squad rule: one flip shifts the
            moment the squad fires, so for a while the two runs disagree everywhere, and then agree again once
            both have synchronised, or never, if only one of them did.
          </p>
          <p class="meta">
            Keys once a figure has focus: space plays and pauses, → or PageDown steps, ← or PageUp undoes a
            tick, r resets to the starting state, f toggles fullscreen. The address bar follows the settings
            of every figure, so a set-up can be linked.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .tabbed { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 10rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0 2rem; align-items: start; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .tabs { position: sticky; top: 1rem; min-width: 0; }
  .tabs ul { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.15rem; border-left: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .tabs a {
    display: block; padding: 0.3rem 0.8rem; margin-left: -1px; border-left: 2px solid transparent;
    color: var(--muted); text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .tabs a:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  .tabs a[aria-selected="true"] { color: var(--ink); border-left-color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
  .panels { min-width: 0; }
  [role="tabpanel"]:focus { outline: none; }
  [role="tabpanel"] > .measure > h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .formula { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.9rem; padding: 0.5rem 0.8rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--line); }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .tabbed { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem; }
    .tabs { top: 0; z-index: 5; background: var(--bg); padding: 0.4rem 0; }
    .tabs ul { flex-direction: row; overflow-x: auto; border-left: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); gap: 0; }
    .tabs a { white-space: nowrap; margin: 0 0 -1px; border-left: 0; border-bottom: 2px solid transparent; padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem; }
    .tabs a[aria-selected="true"] { border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // The hash picks the tab: a tab's own id, or the id of anything inside a tab. Arrow keys walk the list.
  const tabs = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('[role="tab"]'));
  const panels = tabs.map((t) => document.getElementById(t.getAttribute('aria-controls')!)!);
  const box = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.tabbed')!;

  function show(id: string, scroll: boolean) {
    let i = tabs.findIndex((t) => t.getAttribute('aria-controls') === id), target: HTMLElement | null = null;
    if (i < 0) { target = id ? document.getElementById(id) : null; const p = target?.closest<HTMLElement>('[role="tabpanel"]'); i = p ? panels.indexOf(p) : 0; }
    if (i < 0) i = 0;
    tabs.forEach((t, k) => { t.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(k === i)); t.tabIndex = k === i ? 0 : -1; panels[k].hidden = k !== i; });
    // an anchor inside a tab scrolls to it; a tab change only scrolls back up when the reader was deep in the previous one
    if (target) target.scrollIntoView({ block: 'start' });
    else if (scroll && scrollY > box.offsetTop) box.scrollIntoView({ block: 'start' });
  }
  const fromHash = () => show(decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1)), true);
  addEventListener('hashchange', fromHash);
  fromHash();

  document.querySelector('[role="tablist"]')!.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
    const k = (e as KeyboardEvent).key, i = tabs.indexOf(document.activeElement as HTMLAnchorElement);
    if (i < 0) return;
    const n = tabs.length, j = k === 'ArrowDown' || k === 'ArrowRight' ? (i + 1) % n : k === 'ArrowUp' || k === 'ArrowLeft' ? (i + n - 1) % n : k === 'Home' ? 0 : k === 'End' ? n - 1 : -1;
    if (j < 0) return;
    e.preventDefault(); location.hash = tabs[j].getAttribute('aria-controls')!; tabs[j].focus();
  });
</script>
